otto@kalikka.jyu.fi (Otto J. Makela) writes:
{O}  I don't know how NetHack uses memory;

On an MSDOS machine the game will probably take ~400k to load and it will
grab all other available memory for overlay swapping.  You don't really want
to run in <512k though.

{O} that is, once I get it started, can it fail later with "ran out of memory"
{O} or something similar?

Yes.  The game sets aside a small amount of memory for allocating as the game
goes on.  The creation of items and monsters on a level all takes small
amounts of memory to manage.  Once the level is changed all of this is
saved to a file (levels.*).  But it is possible to crash the game by
"piling" stuff up on a level.

{O}  I haven't figured out how to disable shell escapes, not good on bare
{O}   MS-DOS where there is *no* file protection available
{O}   (this would seem to be a simple FAQ question, but...)

Carl Schelin suggested a "quick and dirty" fix to this a while back.  That
was to "set COMSPEC=" before starting nethack out of a batch file.  If
this environment variable is null then the game will not be able to be
shelled out of.  You can also recompile the game sans the SHELL #define
to omit the option too.

Hope this helps.

